Here's a Java method that takes a `List<Car>` and returns the count and color of the most popular `Car.getColor`:

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CarStats {
    public static class CarColorStats {
        private final int count;
        private final String color;

        public CarColorStats(int count, String color) {
            this.count = count;
            this.color = color;
        }

        public int getCount() {
            return count;
        }

        public String getColor() {
            return color;
        }
    }

    public static CarStats getMostPopularCarColor(List<Car> cars) {
        Map<String, Long> colorCounts = cars.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Car::getColor, Collectors.counting()));

        String mostPopularColor = colorCounts.entrySet().stream()
                .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue())
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .orElse(null);

        return new CarStats((int) colorCounts.getOrDefault(mostPopularColor, 0), mostPopularColor);
    }
}
```

This method uses Java Streams to group the cars by their color and count the occurrences of each color. It then finds the color with the highest count and returns a `CarColorStats` object containing the count and the most popular color.

